---
# A gone row (and a failed row nothing reapplies) is where a handle
# ends: that is rest, not a deadlock.
deadlock_detection: false
---

# A project session's orb handle inside the child (plugins/orb): the
# "orb" / "orb-state" service the orb row provides from the moment it
# mounts, while the start behind it may still sync, build and start the
# container. The row's Apply checks the runtime, runs Prepare (repos,
# worktrees, chdir) synchronously, then starts the container on its own
# goroutine (or waits for it, in a headless one-turn run); tools.bash,
# write and patch wait on the handle's ready channel with their own ctx;
# /orb stop cancels a start in flight or stops the open orb; a reload
# with the same config stashes the handle for the next Apply; an unmount
# cancels and waits up to a minute before stopping what it opened.
# orb_lifecycle models state.json and serve; this is the in-process
# handle, one session, one handle.
#
# Where the code disagreed with the mapped model (and the code won):
#   - "HotReloadChangedCfg" never reaches a reuse decision: the row's
#     Effect asks reloading(ctx, cfg), which is true only when a desired
#     row still carries the SAME config, so a changed config closes the
#     handle in the Effect (exactly Unmount below) and the next Apply
#     finds nothing stashed and starts a new handle, which is this spec's
#     Mount again. A stashed handle is therefore always reused, unless
#     the next Apply fails its runtime check first (ReApplyUnavailable).
#   - Prepare failing and chdir failing (Abort) are one outcome: both
#     write state.json failed and hand start() an error it settles with
#     at once; neither fails the row. Only rt.Available fails the row.
#   - A cancelled start can still settle WITH an orb: when the runtime
#     ignores the cancel and starts the container, resumeLocked runs on
#     the cancelled ctx, fails, and Start returns nil, so the handle
#     settles (o, nil) with state.json failed and the container running
#     (res "setup").
#   - /orb stop on a handle whose start failed (no orb) returns nil:
#     "orb stopped; the next command starts it again", which is false.
#   - A waiter woken by the settle runs Orb().Command, whose
#     ensureRunningLocked restarts a stopped container, also after the
#     row's close stopped it (see KNOWN GAPS).
#
# Fields (role Handle):
#   row      the orb row: init | applying (inside Apply) | mounted |
#            failed (Apply returned an error) | stashed (a same-config
#            reload's Effect ran, the next Apply has not) | closing
#            (close() waiting on ready) | gone
#   mode     async (tui, web child: go start()) | headless (a person's
#            or script's one-turn run: start() inside Apply)
#   start    none | preparing | running (start() in flight) | settled
#   prep_err Prepare or chdir failed: start() settles with that error
#   res      how the start settled: "" | ok | setup (orb up, resume.sh
#            failed) | fail | cancel (the ctx error)
#   cancelled  the start's ctx was cancelled (/orb stop, close)
#   ctr      the container this handle starts: missing | running | stopped
#   file     state.json status: none | starting | running | failed | stopped
#   cur      the live Apply reused a stashed handle (reused) or not (fresh)
#   pending  the live Apply's settled() has not run yet (its watcher waits)
#   sec      the "orb" prompt section: "" | starting | ready | failed
#   said     start-failure notices said to the person
#   waiter   one tools call: none | waiting (on Ready); what a finished
#            call got is not kept (the container one shows in exec_on)
#   exec_on  where the last command ran: "" | container | host
#   reply    the last /orb stop reply: "" | ok | ok_no_orb (said stopped
#            with nothing stopped) | err_starting (errStarting after it
#            cancelled)
#   closes   times ready was closed
#   timedout close() gave up waiting after its minute
#   stash    the handle sits in opened.m with no Apply owning it
#   late     a waiter ran a command after the row stopped owning the handle

role Handle:
    action Init:
        self.row = "init"
        self.mode = "async"
        self.start = "none"
        self.prep_err = False
        self.res = ""
        self.cancelled = False
        self.ctr = "missing"
        self.file = "none"
        self.cur = "fresh"
        self.pending = False
        self.sec = ""
        self.said = 0
        self.waiter = "none"
        self.exec_on = ""
        self.reply = ""
        self.closes = 0
        self.timedout = False
        self.stash = False
        self.late = False

    # The part of an Apply after the handle is decided: settled() at
    # once on a settled handle, else the starting section and a watcher.
    atomic func finish_apply(reused):
        self.row = "mounted"
        self.cur = "reused" if reused else "fresh"
        if self.start == "settled":
            self.pending = False
            self.run_settled()
        else:
            self.pending = True
            self.sec = "starting"

    # settled(): the section, and the notice only from the Apply whose
    # start it was.
    atomic func run_settled():
        if has_orb(self):
            self.sec = "ready"
            if self.res == "setup" and self.cur == "fresh":
                self.said = self.said + 1
        else:
            self.sec = "failed"
            if self.cur == "fresh":
                self.said = self.said + 1

    atomic func settle(res, ctr, file):
        self.start = "settled"
        self.res = res
        self.ctr = ctr
        self.file = file
        self.closes = self.closes + 1

    # A tools call past Ready: the orb's Command (restarting a stopped
    # container first), or a "false" command carrying the start's error.
    atomic func run_cmd():
        self.reply = ""
        self.waiter = "none"
        if has_orb(self):
            self.ctr = "running"
            self.file = "running"
            self.exec_on = "container"
            if self.row != "mounted":
                self.late = True

    # The Apply's Effects: gone closed (the watcher and OnResume stop),
    # the section cleared.
    atomic func dispose():
        self.pending = False
        self.sec = ""

    # ---- Apply ----

    atomic action Mount:
        require self.row == "init"
        self.mode = oneof ["async", "headless"]
        self.row = "applying"

    # rt.Available fails before Prepare: the row fails and nothing is
    # written, so no orb shows at all (a finding, see notes).
    atomic action RuntimeUnavailable:
        require self.row == "applying" and self.start == "none"
        self.row = "failed"

    atomic fair action RuntimeOk:
        require self.row == "applying" and self.start == "none"
        self.start = "preparing"
        self.file = "starting"

    # Prepare's repos and worktrees, then the chdir, synchronously in
    # Apply; after it async mode returns the row at once.
    atomic fair action PrepareOk:
        require self.row == "applying" and self.start == "preparing"
        self.start = "running"
        if self.mode == "async":
            self.finish_apply(False)

    # Prepare or chdir failed (state.json failed): start() settles with
    # the error at once, on its goroutine in async mode.
    atomic fair action PrepareFail:
        require self.row == "applying" and self.start == "preparing"
        self.start = "running"
        self.prep_err = True
        self.file = "failed"
        if self.mode == "async":
            self.finish_apply(False)

    # ---- the start (o.Start on octx) ----

    atomic fair action StartOk:
        require self.start == "running" and not self.prep_err and not self.cancelled
        self.settle("ok", "running", "running")

    atomic action ResumeScriptFails:
        require self.start == "running" and not self.prep_err and not self.cancelled
        self.settle("setup", "running", "failed")

    atomic fair action StartFail:
        require self.start == "running" and (self.prep_err or not self.cancelled)
        self.settle("fail", "missing", "failed")

    # The cancel reaches the build or the runtime: settle(nil, ctx err).
    atomic fair action CancelHonored:
        require self.start == "running" and not self.prep_err and self.cancelled
        self.settle("cancel", "missing", "failed")

    # The runtime starts the container anyway; resume.sh runs on the dead
    # ctx and fails; Start returns nil.
    atomic action RuntimeIgnoresCancel:
        require self.start == "running" and not self.prep_err and self.cancelled
        self.settle("setup", "running", "failed")

    # A headless run waited for the start inside Apply: a failed start or
    # a failed resume.sh closes the handle and fails the row with the
    # report; otherwise the row mounts on the open orb.
    atomic fair action HeadlessNonWebRun:
        require self.row == "applying" and self.mode == "headless" and self.start == "settled"
        if self.res == "ok":
            self.finish_apply(False)
        else:
            self.said = self.said + 1
            if has_orb(self):
                self.ctr = "stopped"
                self.file = "stopped"
            self.row = "failed"

    # The live Apply's watcher sees ready closed.
    atomic fair action Notify:
        require self.row == "mounted" and self.pending and self.start == "settled"
        self.pending = False
        self.run_settled()

    # ---- callers ----

    atomic action CallerExec:
        require self.row == "mounted" and self.waiter != "waiting"
        if self.start == "settled":
            self.run_cmd()
        else:
            self.waiter = "waiting"
            self.reply = ""

    # Ready's select sees ready closed. The call holds the handle, so
    # this runs whatever became of the row meanwhile.
    atomic fair action WaiterWakes:
        require self.waiter == "waiting" and self.start == "settled"
        self.run_cmd()

    atomic action CallerCtxTimeout:
        require self.waiter == "waiting"
        self.waiter = "none"

    # ---- /orb stop (no jobs running, or force) ----

    # Stop cancels and returns errStarting, which /orb prints as an error.
    atomic action SlashOrbStopWhileStarting:
        require self.row == "mounted" and self.start != "settled"
        self.cancelled = True
        self.reply = "err_starting"

    atomic action SlashOrbStopWhenReady:
        require self.row == "mounted" and self.start == "settled"
        if has_orb(self):
            self.ctr = "stopped"
            self.file = "stopped"
            self.reply = "ok"
        else:
            self.reply = "ok_no_orb"

    # ---- reloads and unmount ----

    # The same config is still desired: the Effect stashes the handle.
    atomic action HotReloadSameCfg:
        require self.row == "mounted"
        self.dispose()
        self.row = "stashed"
        self.stash = True

    # The next Apply fails its runtime check before it looks in opened:
    # the stashed handle stays there, owned by no row.
    atomic action ReApplyUnavailable:
        require self.row == "stashed"
        self.row = "failed"

    # The next Apply (or a later retry of a failed one) reuses it.
    atomic fair action ReloadReused:
        require self.row == "stashed" or (self.row == "failed" and self.stash)
        self.stash = False
        self.finish_apply(True)

    # Row removed, session ending, or HotReloadChangedCfg (see header):
    # the Effects, then close(): cancel, wait for ready.
    atomic action Unmount:
        require self.row == "mounted" or self.row == "failed"
        if self.row == "mounted":
            self.dispose()
            self.cancelled = True
            self.row = "closing"
        else:
            self.row = "gone"

    atomic fair action CloseSettled:
        require self.row == "closing" and self.start == "settled"
        if has_orb(self):
            self.ctr = "stopped"
            self.file = "stopped"
        self.row = "gone"

    # close()'s time.After(time.Minute): the start is abandoned.
    atomic action CloseTimeout:
        require self.row == "closing" and self.start != "settled"
        self.timedout = True
        self.row = "gone"

action Init:
    h = Handle()

# The handle holds an open orb: the start settled with one.
def has_orb(x):
    return x.start == "settled" and x.res in ("ok", "setup")

# h.State(): the orb's own state once open, else state.json (Prepare
# writes it before the handle exists), else "starting".
def state_of(x):
    if x.file == "none":
        return "starting"
    return x.file

# A command runs in the container or not at all.
always assertion NeverOnHost:
    return h.exec_on != "host"

# settle is the only close(ready), and it runs once.
always assertion ReadyClosedOnce:
    return h.closes == (1 if h.start == "settled" else 0)

# Once the row is gone the container is down, except through the three
# leaks in KNOWN GAPS (each named by the field it sets).
always assertion NoLeakAfterUnmountExceptKnown:
    return h.row != "gone" or h.ctr != "running" or h.timedout or h.stash or h.late

# Each start's outcome is said once, by the Apply whose start it was.
always assertion NoReannounce:
    return h.said <= 1

always assertion StateNeverFabricatesRunning:
    return state_of(h) != "running" or h.ctr == "running"

# /orb stop's "orb stopped" leaves nothing running (until a command
# starts it again, which clears the reply).
always assertion StopOkMeansStopped:
    return h.reply not in ("ok", "ok_no_orb") or h.ctr != "running"

# Every handle's start settles (the start goroutine makes progress).
always eventually assertion EverySettles:
    return h.start in ("none", "settled")

# Every waiter gets the start's result or its own ctx error.
always eventually assertion WaitersAnswered:
    return h.waiter != "waiting"

# A cancelled start still ends up in front of the model: the failed
# section (or a notice), while the row is up.
always eventually assertion CancelIsReported:
    return h.row != "mounted" or h.res != "cancel" or h.sec == "failed"

# KNOWN GAPS: the product as it is, modelled faithfully above; the
# property it ought to have is not asserted, since fizz fails each
# (added to a copy of this spec; the trace fizz gave is shown).
#
# 1. NoLeakAfterUnmount: a container this handle started outlives the
#    row three ways. (a) close() gives up after a minute and the start
#    settles later with the container up (the runtime ignored the
#    cancel); nobody stops it. (b) A same-config reload stashes the
#    handle and the next Apply fails rt.Available before it takes it
#    out of opened.m: no row owns it, the unmount stops nothing.
#    (c) A tools call waiting on Ready wakes after close() stopped the
#    orb and ensureRunningLocked starts it again.
#      always assertion NoLeakAfterUnmount:
#          return h.row != "gone" or h.ctr != "running"
#    fizz (a): Mount(async), RuntimeOk, PrepareOk, Unmount, CloseTimeout,
#    RuntimeIgnoresCancel. (b), with timedout exempt: ..., PrepareOk,
#    StartOk, HotReloadSameCfg, ReApplyUnavailable, Unmount. (c), with
#    timedout and stash exempt: ..., PrepareOk, CallerExec, StartOk,
#    Unmount, CloseSettled, WaiterWakes.
#
# 2. StopHonest: /orb stop while starting cancels the start and then
#    returns errStarting, printed as a failure; on a failed start it
#    says "orb stopped; the next command starts it again" although
#    there was nothing to stop and every next command fails.
#      always assertion StopHonest:
#          return h.reply in ("", "ok")
#    fizz: Mount(async), RuntimeOk, PrepareOk, SlashOrbStopWhileStarting.
#
# 3. CancelRecovers: settle(nil, ctx err) is final. After /orb stop
#    mid-start every tools.bash fails with "context canceled" for the
#    rest of the session (a same-config reload reuses the failed
#    handle), and the report is the generic "Check the project's repos
#    and the container runtime", which does not say to restart it.
#      always eventually assertion CancelRecovers:
#          return h.row != "mounted" or h.res != "cancel"
#    fizz: Mount(async), RuntimeOk, PrepareOk, CallerExec,
#    SlashOrbStopWhileStarting, CancelHonored, Notify, WaiterWakes (and
#    so on forever).
#
# 4. FailureSaidToPerson: a reload before the start settles makes the
#    reused Apply the watcher, and a reused Apply never says the
#    failure; the Apply whose start it was is gone. Only the prompt
#    section carries it. The same after the settle, before its watcher
#    ran.
#      always eventually assertion FailureSaidToPerson:
#          return h.row != "mounted" or h.res not in ("fail", "cancel") or h.said >= 1
#    fizz: Mount(async), RuntimeOk, PrepareOk, StartFail,
#    SlashOrbStopWhenReady, HotReloadSameCfg, ReApplyUnavailable,
#    ReloadReused.
